#header:
  #-: Zerolang Type Representation

#nav id=toc class=toc-sidebar:
  #div id=tocfile: Table of Contents
  #doc.toc


This document specifies the memory representation and allocation strategy for
all types in Zerolang.


#-: Overview

#--: Design Principles

#ol:
  [#*: #+ Stack by default. Every user-defined type -- record, class, variant,
  union, protocol instance, facet instance -- lives on the stack. The struct
  that describes the object is a local variable or a field inside another
  struct. No #~"malloc" / #~"free" for the struct itself.]
  [#*: #+ Heap for data buffers only. The only heap allocations are
  #~"Box" (a single object on the heap), #~"List" data buffers, #~"String"
  data buffers, and union subtype data. The controlling struct for #~"List"
  and #~"String" (length, capacity, pointer) still lives on the stack.]
  [#*: #+ Ownership tied to type class. Valtypes (records, variants) are
  freely copyable. Reftypes (classes, unions, protocol instances) are
  single-owner and cannot be copied -- only borrowed or taken.]
  [#*: #+ Flat valtypes. Valtypes may only contain other valtypes as fields.
  They cannot contain #~"String", #~"List", #~"Box", or any other type that
  requires heap cleanup. This guarantees that valtypes are always trivially
  copyable and never need a destructor.]
  [#*: #+ Reftypes may own heap resources. Reftypes (classes, unions, protocol
  instances) may contain #~"String", #~"List", #~"Box", or other reftype
  fields. However, a reftype is not required to own heap resources -- a class
  with only valtype fields is valid. Such a class still has identity and
  single-owner semantics but needs no destructor.]
  [#*: #+ Locks live on reftypes. The non-owned field mechanism is available to
  reftypes (classes) only. Valtypes (records, variants) cannot hold locks.
  Born-borrowed is removed as a concept.]


#--: Type Classification Summary

#table cols="3 2 2 2 2 2":
  Type                |Stack/Heap     |Copyable |Destructor          |Can have heap fields |non-owned fields
  record              |stack          |yes      |no                  |no                   |no
  variant             |stack          |yes      |no                  |no                   |no
  class               |stack          |no       |if heap fields      |yes                  |yes
  union               |stack (struct) |no       |yes (frees subdata) |yes (in subtypes)    |no
  protocol instance   |stack (struct) |no       |if wrapping reftype |yes (via wrapped)    |no
  facet instance      |stack          |yes      |no                  |no                   |no
  box(T)              |heap (data)    |no       |yes (free data)     |n/a                  |no
  list(T) (both families) |stack (struct) |no    |yes (free data buf) |n/a                  |no
  set / map (all forms) |heap (struct)  |no     |yes (free tables)   |n/a                  |no
  idmap / idset         |stack (struct) |no      |yes (free arrays)   |n/a                  |no
  string              |stack (struct) |no       |yes (free data buf) |n/a                  |no
  listView(T) (both families) |stack   |no      |no                  |no                   |yes
  stringView          |stack          |no       |no                  |no                   |yes

The container rows cover both element families: #~"ListRef" /
#~"ListVal", #~"SetRef" / #~"SetVal", the four #~"Map" forms and the two
#~"IdMap" forms share one C shape per kind -- the families differ in what
the element slots hold (owned reftype pointers vs inline valtype values)
and in the accessor/iterator surface, not in the container struct itself.

#~"Map" and #~"Set" are the one heap-allocated container: #~"create"
returns a pointer, so a map-typed field holds a pointer the owner
destroys rather than storage the owner contains. #~"IdMap" / #~"IdSet"
are stack structs like #~"List", which is what lets an empty one cost no
allocation at all.

Note: "enum" is not a separate type. Enums are variants or unions where every
subtype is #~"null" -- the struct collapses to just a tag discriminator.


#--: Method Receiver (#~"this")

For all type instances with methods, #~"this" is always passed as a pointer
at the C level -- #~"T*" for a mutable (#~".borrow") receiver, #~"const T*"
for a readonly (#~".view") receiver. This applies to records, classes,
unions, and any other type with methods. The pointer convention ensures:

#ul:
  [#*: Methods can mutate fields and have those mutations persist across calls.]
  [#*: Method bodies work identically for stack-allocated and boxed instances --
  #~"Box(T)" is already a pointer, so calling a method on a boxed value passes
  the pointer directly without #~"&".]

At call sites, the emitter generates #~"&var" to pass the address of a stack
variable to a method:

#code language=c: """
  /* zerolang: counter.increment c */
  z_counter_increment(&zv_c);
  """


#-: Type Specifications


#--: Records (Valtypes)

Records are product value types. They are stack-allocated, freely copyable, and
never need a destructor.

#---: Memory Layout

A record is a C struct with all fields inline:

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      int64_t x;
      int64_t y;
  } z_point_t;
  """

#---: Field Restrictions

Record fields must be valtypes: numeric types, bools, other records, variants,
function references. Records #_*"cannot" contain #~"String", #~"List",
#~"Box", #~"Map", classes, unions, or any type that requires heap cleanup.
This restriction is enforced by the type checker.

Records cannot have non-owned fields. Locks require identity (single-owner
semantics) which valtypes do not have. See the Born-Borrowed Analysis section
for discussion.

#---: Construction

#~"meta.create" returns the struct by value:

#code language=c: """
  static z_point_t z_point_meta_create(int64_t x, int64_t y) {
      z_point_t _this = {0};
      _this.x = x;
      _this.y = y;
      return _this;
  }
  """

#---: Ownership

Records are freely copied on assignment and passed by value to functions. Each
copy is independent. When a record falls out of scope it is simply forgotten --
no destructor runs.


#--: Variants (Valtypes)

Variants are sum value types (tagged unions). They are stack-allocated with
inline subtype data, freely copyable, and never need a destructor.

#---: Memory Layout

A variant uses an inline C union sized to the largest subtype:

#code language=c: """
  typedef enum {
      Z_SHAPE_TAG_CIRCLE,
      Z_SHAPE_TAG_RECT,
  } z_shape_tag_t;

  typedef struct {
      z_shape_tag_t tag;
      union {
          double circle;            /* radius */
          z_rect_t rect;            /* inline record */
      } data;
  } z_shape_t;
  """

The struct size is #~"sizeof(tag) + sizeof(largest subtype)". For enum-pattern
variants (all subtypes are #~"null"), the union is omitted entirely and the
struct is just #~"{ tag }".

#---: Field Restrictions

Variant subtypes must be valtypes. They cannot contain #~"String", #~"List",
#~"Box", classes, unions, or any type requiring heap cleanup. This ensures
variants are trivially copyable and need no destructor.

#---: Ownership

Variants are freely copied (struct copy captures tag + active data). No
destructor is needed. When a variant falls out of scope, it is forgotten.


#--: Classes (Reftypes)

Classes are product reference types. They are #+ stack-allocated. They have
single-owner semantics: they cannot be copied, only borrowed or taken.

#---: Memory Layout

A class is a C struct with all fields inline, stored on the stack:

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      z_string_t label;    /* string struct (stack), data buffer (heap) */
      int64_t value;
  } z_named_t;
  """

There is no #~"malloc" for the struct itself. The struct lives in the stack
frame of the declaring scope.

#---: Field Types

Classes may contain any field type: valtypes, other reftypes, #~"String",
#~"List", #~"Box", and #~"Map". A class is not required to have heap-backed
fields -- a class with only valtype fields is valid. Such a class still has
identity and single-owner semantics (cannot be copied) but needs no destructor.

#---: Construction

#~"meta.create" returns the struct by value (not a pointer):

#code language=c: """
  static z_named_t z_named_meta_create(z_string_t label, int64_t value) {
      z_named_t _this = {0};
      _this.label = label;
      _this.value = value;
      return _this;
  }
  """

At the call site, the class variable is a stack local:

#code language=c: """
  z_named_t n = z_named_create(z_string_new("test"), 42);
  """

#---: Destructor

A class destructor is emitted only if the class has fields that require
cleanup (string, list, box, map, or other reftypes with destructors). The
destructor cleans up those fields but does #_*"not" free the struct itself:

#code language=c: """
  static void z_named_destroy(z_named_t* p) {
      if (!p) return;
      z_string_free(&p->label);   /* free string data buffer */
      /* NO free(p) -- struct is on the stack */
  }
  """

If a class has no heap-backed fields (only valtypes), no destructor is emitted
and the variable is not added to the scope cleanup list.

#---: Ownership Transfer (.take)

Taking a class copies the struct (C struct assignment) and zero-initialises the
source. Zero-initialisation sets all pointer fields to #~"NULL", preventing
double-free when the source's scope cleanup runs:

#code language=c: """
  z_named_t d = zv_c;                         /* struct copy */
  zv_c = (z_named_t){0};                      /* invalidate source */
  """

#---: .drop

#~".drop" calls the destructor (if present) and zero-initialises the struct:

#code language=c: """
  z_named_destroy(&zv_n);
  zv_n = (z_named_t){0};
  """

#---: Scope Cleanup

When an owned class variable falls out of scope, the destructor is called (if
the type has one). The cleanup code passes #~"&var" to the destructor:

#code language=c: """
  z_named_destroy(&zv_n);
  """

#---: Function Returns

Functions returning a class return the struct by value. C compilers apply NRVO
(Named Return Value Optimisation) in practice, avoiding unnecessary copies.

#---: Parameter Passing

Borrow parameters receive a pointer (the caller passes #~"&var"); view
parameters receive a #~"const" pointer. Take parameters receive the struct
by value (the caller copies the struct and zero-initialises its source);
#~".hold" is take with a readonly binding, and an #~"outx" declaration marks
a return that pins its non-owned arguments.

#---: Swap

Swapping two class variables swaps the entire struct:

#code language=c: """
  {
      z_named_t _tmp = zv_a;
      zv_a = zv_b;
      zv_b = _tmp;
  }
  """

#---: Non-Owned Fields on Classes

Classes may have non-owned fields. A class with non-owned fields:

#ul:
  [#*: Holds compile-time locks on the targets of its non-owned fields for the
  lifetime of the class instance.]
  [#*: Cannot be stored as a field of another type (prevents the lock from
  outliving its target).]
  [#*: Can be moved via #~".take" -- ownership and locks transfer to the new
  name.]
  [#*: Can be passed to #~".borrow" and non-owned parameters.]
  [#*: When the instance falls out of scope, locks are released (and the
  destructor runs if present).]

Classes with non-owned fields are normal owned instances. The single-owner
semantics of classes (no copying) already provide the safety guarantees that
born-borrowed previously enforced for records. A class cannot be duplicated, so
there is no risk of multiple copies holding the same lock.

non-owned fields are immutable after construction.


#--: Unions (Reftypes)

Unions are sum reference types. The struct itself is #+ stack-allocated; the
subtype data is heap-allocated via a #~"void*" pointer.

#---: Memory Layout

A union is always two pointer-widths (16 bytes): a tag and a #~"void*":

#code language=c: """
  typedef enum {
      Z_RESULT_TAG_OK,
      Z_RESULT_TAG_ERR,
  } z_result_tag_t;

  typedef struct {
      z_result_tag_t tag;
      void* data;           /* heap-allocated subtype data */
  } z_result_t;
  """

The struct is 16 bytes on the stack regardless of subtype sizes. Subtype data
is heap-allocated and owned by the union.

#---: Subtype Data

A union's arms hold reftypes: a #~"null" arm carries no payload, a marked
arm (#~".view" / #~".borrow" / #~".hold") is reference-family whatever it
points at, and a valtype arm is spelled #~"(Box T)" -- which collapses to
the element, so the representation below is unchanged by the rule. The
subtype data is always heap-allocated:

#ul:
  [#*: For valtype subtypes: #~"malloc(sizeof(subtype))" + copy value.]
  [#*: For reftype subtypes (classes, strings, etc.): #~"malloc(sizeof(subtype))"
  + move (take) the struct into the heap allocation, invalidating the source.]
  [#*: For #~"null" subtypes: #~"data" is #~"NULL".]

Because union subtypes can be reftypes, a union may indirectly contain
#~"String", #~"List", #~"Box", and other heap-backed fields within its
subtypes.

#---: Destructor

The destructor switches on the tag, cleans up the active subtype, frees the
subtype data, but does #_*"not" free the union struct:

#code language=c: """
  static void z_result_destroy(z_result_t* u) {
      if (!u) return;
      switch (u->tag) {
          case Z_RESULT_TAG_OK:
              break;
          case Z_RESULT_TAG_ERR:
              z_string_free((z_string_t*)u->data);
              free(u->data);
              break;
      }
  }
  """

A union always needs a destructor if any subtype has non-null data (to free the
heap allocation for the subtype data).

#---: Construction

Subtype construction allocates and stores the data:

#code language=c: """
  z_result_t r = {0};
  r.tag = Z_RESULT_TAG_OK;
  r.data = malloc(sizeof(int64_t));
  *(int64_t*)r.data = 42;
  """

For null subtypes:

#code language=c: """
  z_result_t r = {0};
  r.tag = Z_RESULT_TAG_ERR;
  r.data = NULL;
  """

#---: Match / Case

Match arms cast #~"data" to the subtype:

#code language=c: """
  switch (zv_r.tag) {
      case Z_RESULT_TAG_OK:
          printf("%lld\n", *(int64_t*)zv_r.data);
          break;
      case Z_RESULT_TAG_ERR:
          z_string_free((z_string_t*)zv_r.data);
          break;
  }
  """


#--: Protocol Instances

Protocol instances are existential type wrappers. The wrapper struct is
#+ stack-allocated. The wrapped data may be a borrowed
pointer or an owned heap copy.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      void (*method1)(void*, ...);
      void (*method2)(void*, ...);
      ...
  } z_printable_vtable_t;

  typedef struct {
      void* data;                       /* pointer to implementation */
      z_printable_vtable_t* vtable;     /* pointer to static vtable */
      void (*destroy)(void*);           /* destructor (NULL if borrowed) */
  } z_printable_t;
  """

The protocol struct (3 pointers = 24 bytes) is on the stack.

#---: Borrowed vs Owned

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Borrowed (#~"protocol.borrow"): #~"data" points to the source
  variable (via #~"&var"). #~"destroy" is #~"NULL". The protocol instance does
  not own the data.]
  [#*: #+ Owned (#~"protocol.create"): #~"data" points to a heap-allocated
  copy of the implementation. #~"destroy" is set to a cleanup function that
  frees the copy. For reftype implementations, the cleanup function calls the
  implementation's destructor before freeing.]

#---: Destructor

The protocol destructor calls #~"destroy" if set, then does #_*"not" free the
protocol struct (it is on the stack):

#code language=c: """
  static void z_printable_destroy(z_printable_t* p) {
      if (!p) return;
      if (p->destroy) p->destroy(p->data);
      /* NO free(p) -- struct is on the stack */
  }
  """

Since the wrapped data may be a reftype that contains #~"String", #~"List",
#~"Box", etc., protocol instances may indirectly own heap resources.


#--: Facet Instances

Facet instances are value-type existential wrappers. They are stack-allocated
with inline data and are freely copyable.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef union {
      z_impl1_t _impl1;
      z_impl2_t _impl2;
  } z_iterable_data_u;

  typedef struct {
      z_iterable_vtable_t* vtable;      /* pointer to static vtable */
      z_iterable_data_u data;            /* inline data union */
  } z_iterable_t;
  """

The data is stored inline (not via pointer). The facet struct size is
#~"sizeof(vtable pointer) + sizeof(largest conforming type)".

#---: Restrictions

Because facets are valtypes, their implementations must be valtypes (records,
variants). They cannot wrap reftypes (classes, unions) and therefore cannot
indirectly contain #~"String", #~"List", #~"Box", or other heap-backed fields.
No destructor is needed.


#--: box(T)

#~"Box" is a generic type that heap-allocates a single instance of a
#_*"valtype" (its bound is #~"anyval.generic"; a reftype is already a heap
pointer). The C representation is a bare pointer to the inner type. As a
union arm or union type argument, #~"(Box T)" collapses to the element --
see the union section.

#---: Memory Layout

For #~"Box(point)" where point is #~"{ i64 x, i64 y }":

#code language=c: """
  /* C type: z_point_t*  (pointer to heap-allocated point struct) */
  z_point_t* b = (z_point_t*)malloc(sizeof(z_point_t));
  *b = z_point_create(1, 2);
  """

#---: Transparency

Boxed items support all field and method access directly. The compiler handles
the indirection. There is no automatic unboxing: passing a boxed item to a
function requires the function to accept exactly that boxed type.

Since #~"this" is always a pointer in methods, all method bodies work on both
boxed (heap pointer) and unboxed (stack, via #~"&var") instances without
modification.

#---: Destructor

The box destructor frees the heap allocation. A boxed element is a
valtype, and valtypes own no heap, so the destructor is #~"free(v)":

#code language=c: """
  static void z_Box_pt_destroy(void* v) {
      if (!v) return;
      free(v);               /* free the box allocation */
  }
  """

#---: Born-Boxed Construction

When a type is defined as a boxed type at the unit level, construction should
allocate directly on the heap and construct in-place. This avoids the overhead
of constructing on the stack then copying to the heap.

The box's #~"meta.create" does the heap allocation and field initialisation
in one step:

#code language=c: """
  static z_point_t* z_box_point_meta_create(int64_t x, int64_t y) {
      z_point_t* _this = (z_point_t*)malloc(sizeof(z_point_t));
      *_this = (z_point_t){0};
      _this->x = x;
      _this->y = y;
      return _this;
  }
  """

This is the heap-allocating version of #~"meta.create" -- the same pattern
currently used for classes, now repurposed for box types only.

#---: Boxing a Pre-Existing Item

When boxing a variable that already exists:

#code language=zerolang: """
  p: point x: 1 y: 2
  b: Box from: p
  """

The compiler generates: allocate on heap, copy the value. The source is a
valtype, so it is copied, not consumed -- the standard valtype rule.

#---: Swap

Swapping two boxed items of the same type is a pointer swap:

#code language=c: """
  {
      z_point_t* _tmp = zv_a;
      zv_a = zv_b;
      zv_b = _tmp;
  }
  """

Swapping a boxed item with an unboxed item of the same underlying type is
#_*"not" supported. This would require a move-in/move-out dance that is
error-prone. The programmer should unbox/rebox explicitly if needed.


#--: list(T)

#~"List" (the alias of #~"ListRef"; #~"ListVal" is the valtype-element
twin with the identical layout) is a generic growable array. The struct
lives on the stack; only the data buffer is heap-allocated. Emitted C
names are id-mangled (#~"z_t1014_t"); the readable spellings in this
document's samples correspond to the opt-in readable-names emitter mode.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      uint64_t length;
      int64_t* data;        /* heap-allocated data buffer */
      uint64_t capacity;
  } z_list_i64_t;
  """

Field order: #~"length" first, #~"data" second, #~"capacity" last. This order
is chosen so that the first two fields (#~"length", #~"data") match the
#~"ListView" layout, enabling zero-cost casting from #~"List*" to
#~"ListView*". Capacity is last because it is not present in views.

The struct (24 bytes) is on the stack. The #~"data" pointer references a
heap-allocated buffer sized to #~"capacity * sizeof(element)".

#---: Construction

#~"create" returns the struct by value (not a pointer):

#code language=c: """
  static z_list_i64_t z_list_i64_create(uint64_t _capacity) {
      z_list_i64_t _this = {0};
      _this.capacity = _capacity;
      if (_capacity > 0) {
          _this.data = (int64_t*)calloc(_capacity, sizeof(int64_t));
      }
      return _this;
  }
  """

#---: Destructor

The destructor frees the data buffer (and element destructors for reftype
elements). It does #_*"not" free the struct:

#code language=c: """
  static void z_list_i64_destroy(z_list_i64_t* p) {
      if (!p) return;
      free(p->data);
      /* NO free(p) -- struct is on the stack */
  }
  """

For reftype elements:

#code language=c: """
  static void z_list_string_destroy(z_list_string_t* p) {
      if (!p) return;
      for (uint64_t i = 0; i < p->length; i++) {
          z_string_free(&p->data[i]);
      }
      free(p->data);
  }
  """

#---: Growth Formula

Unchanged: #~"new_cap = cap + (cap >> 1) + 4" (approximately 1.5x + 4).

#---: Methods

All methods (append, insert, extend, get, set, pop, grow) take #~"this" as a
pointer. Call sites pass #~"&var".

#---: In-Place Element Creation

For creating elements directly in the data buffer (avoiding a stack temporary
+ copy), two approaches are possible:

#ol:
  [#*: #+ Append-then-init: append a zero-initialised element, then return a
  pointer to the last slot for the caller to initialise.]
  [#*: #+ Placement-style: provide a variant of append that takes construction
  arguments and constructs the element in-place.]

Recommendation: defer in-place creation to a future optimisation. The current
pattern (construct element on stack, append copies it into data buffer) is
correct and the copy cost is negligible for element-sized data.


#--: listView(T)

#~"ListView" (and #~"ListViewVal", its valtype-element twin) is a generic
window into a list. It is a class (single ownership) whose production pins
the parent list exclusively. It is not read-only: #~"get" is the readonly
accessor and #~"getMut" borrows the element for in-place writes; what a
view cannot do is change the sequence.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      uint64_t length;
      int64_t* data;        /* pointer into parent list's data buffer */
  } z_listview_i64_t;
  """

Field order matches the first two fields of #~"List": #~"length" first,
#~"data" second. This enables zero-cost casting from a #~"z_list_i64_t*" to a
#~"z_listview_i64_t*" -- the memory is reinterpreted without copying.

#---: Ownership

Listview is a class (reftype): single ownership, cannot be copied. It holds a
#~".borrow" on the source list, preventing mutation of the list while the view
exists. When the listView falls out of scope, the lock is released.

Listview does #_*"not" need a destructor (no heap allocations of its own). The
data it points to is owned by the parent list.

#---: Locking

The listView holds a compile-time lock on the source list:

#code language=zerolang: """
  nums: (ListVal i64)
  nums.append from: 10
  nums.append from: 20
  nums.append from: 30
  v: nums.listView           # v locks nums
  print v.get i: 0.u64       # OK: read through view
  # nums.append from: 40     # ERROR: nums is locked by v
  # when v falls out of scope, nums is unlocked
  """

#---: Methods

#ul:
  [#*: #~"get(i: u64)" -- bounds-checked readonly access (#~"of.view" on
    #~"ListView", a copy on #~"ListViewVal")]
  [#*: #~"getMut(i: u64) outx of.borrow" -- exclusive element access]
  [#*: #~"length() out u64" -- number of elements in the view]

#---: Zerolang Definition

#code language=zerolang: """
  ListView: class {
      get: function {t: this.borrow i: u64} outx of.view is native
      getMut: function {t: this.borrow i: u64} outx of.borrow is native
      length: function {t: this.view} out u64 is native
  } as {
      of: AnyRef.generic
  }
  """


#--: map(K,V) / set(T)

#~"Map" (the four key/value families #~"MapRR" / #~"MapRV" / #~"MapVR" /
#~"MapVV") and #~"Set" (#~"SetRef" / #~"SetVal") are compact dictionaries: a
dense, insertion-ordered entry array plus a sparse index table of positions
into it. Iteration order is therefore insertion order and is stable across
runs regardless of the hash seed.

They are the one #_*"heap-allocated" container: #~"create" returns a pointer.
A map-typed field holds a pointer the owner destroys, not storage the owner
contains -- which is why a map field can be read through a readonly receiver
where a stack-struct container's cannot.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      uint8_t  alive;        /* 0 once removed: a tombstone */
      uint64_t hash;         /* stored, so a probe compares before calling ==  */
      K        key;
      V        value;
  } z_map_entry_t;

  typedef struct z_map_t {
      uint64_t capacity;     /* indices slots, a power of two */
      uint64_t length;       /* LIVE entries */
      uint64_t entries_len;  /* entries[] high-water mark: live + tombstones */
      uint64_t entries_cap;  /* entries[] allocation */
      int64_t* indices;      /* EMPTY(-1) / DELETED(-2) / entry position */
      z_map_entry_t* entries;
  } z_map_t;
  """

A set is the same shape without the #~"value" column. #~"length" and
#~"entries_len" differ only because removal leaves a hole: #~"length" counts
live entries, #~"entries_len" counts slots used.

#---: Construction

#~"create" returns a #~"z_map_t*". A capacity under 8 is rounded to zero and
allocates #_*"neither" array; the header itself is always allocated.

#---: Destructor

Frees #~"indices", frees #~"entries" (running the element destructors for
owning key/value families first), then frees the struct.

#---: Growth Formula

The index doubles when #~"(entries_len + 1) * 3 >= capacity * 2" -- a 2/3 load
factor. Growth compacts: tombstones are dropped and the surviving entries are
re-slotted, which is what reclaims them.

#---: Probing

#~"hash & (capacity - 1)", then linear. The stored hash is compared before the
key so a full comparison runs only on a hash match. Integer keys hash through
#~"z_hash_u64" (the splitmix64 finalizer); strings and byte-range keys through
SipHash, or a faster non-cryptographic mixer under #~"--fast-hash".

#---: Methods

#~"get" / #~"set" / #~"has" / #~"remove" / #~"length" / #~"capacity" /
#~"iterate" / #~"iterateItems" / #~"create". There is no #~"getMut": the slot
a key occupies depends on a hash #_*"of that key", so writing through a
mutable view would leave the entry in the wrong bucket.


#--: idmap(K,V) / idset(T)

#~"IdMap" (#~"IdMapR" / #~"IdMapV") and #~"IdSet" are the append-only,
id-keyed siblings. The key is bound to an unsigned scalar by #~"idkey"
(#~"u8" / #~"u16" / #~"u32" / #~"u64" / #~"c8" / #~"c32", and any typedef over
one of them), and there is no #~"remove" and no #~"delete": the whole table is
freed at once.

Three properties follow from that contract, and none is available to a Map.
Nothing is ever removed, so #~"entries[0 .. length-1]" are #_*"all" live and
the array has no holes. The slot is computed from the key alone, so no hash
column is stored. And the struct lives on the #_*"stack", like a #~"List", so
an empty one costs no allocation at all.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      K key;
      V value;
  } z_idmap_entry_t;

  typedef struct z_idmap_t {
      uint32_t  length;      /* live entries; ALSO slots used, ALSO next index */
      uint32_t  capacity;    /* indices slots, a power of two */
      uint32_t* indices;     /* pos + 1, with 0 for empty; NULL in small mode */
      z_idmap_entry_t* entries;
  } z_idmap_t;
  """

24 bytes, the same as a #~"List". An id set is the same shape with a bare
#~"items" array in place of #~"entries".

One counter does three jobs -- live count, slots used, and the next insertion
index -- because there are no holes to tell those numbers apart. #~"indices"
is #~"uint32_t" holding #~"pos + 1" so that zero means empty; with no removal
there is no #~"DELETED" sentinel to distinguish, which is what allows the
narrower cell.

#---: Small Mode

Below an index size of 16 no #~"indices" array is allocated at all and lookup
scans the dense entries instead. #~"indices == NULL" is the discriminator, so
the indexed path pays no extra branch -- it replaces the #~"capacity == 0"
early return a Map's #~"find" already had.

The ceiling is on #~"capacity", which is always a power of two, so the
constant has #_*"four" reachable settings and no others: 8, 16, 32 and 64,
admitting 5, 10, 21 and 42 entries. #b:"16 is the measured optimum" -- 8 is
marginally worse and 32 and 64 cost 1.4% and 3.0% of the compiler's
instructions to save 0.3% and 0.6% of its bytes. This is the one constant in
the container that cannot be chosen from the allocation column, which improves
monotonically as it rises. The scan stays competitive further than the usual
advice about lists and maps suggests, because that advice is about a hashed
map with an owned key and this scan is #~"==" over raw scalars with no hash
and no allocation.

Because this crossing is inside the container, a caller never writes one. A
structure that was a list plus a lazily built by-name index -- with the
threshold test repeated in every reader -- is one field here.

#---: Construction

#~"create" returns the struct #_*"by value". An empty one allocates nothing:
no header, no index, no entries.

#---: Destructor

Frees #~"indices" and #~"entries" (running the value destructors for the
reference family first) and does #_*"not" free the struct -- it is on the
stack, exactly as #~"List" is.

#---: Growth Formula

#~"entries" is sized at #~"capacity * 2 / 3", which is also the index's load
factor, so "entries is full" and "the index must grow" are one event and no
separate #~"entries_cap" field is needed. The test carries no division:
cross-multiplied it is #~"(length + 1) * 3 >= capacity * 2", the same growth
point a Map reaches. Growth doubles the index and re-slots; there is nothing
to compact, because #~"entries" never has holes.

#---: Indexing

#~"(key * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15) >> 32", masked by #~"capacity - 1".

Identity (#~"key & mask") was measured and rejected. It is optimal for a dense
run of ids -- one table's worst probe fell from 74 to 1 -- but a table holding
a #_*"sparse subset" of a wide id space folds unrelated groups onto each other,
because the mask keeps only the low bits: over a self-compile identity cost
+77% probe steps and took the worst run from 74 to 749. The golden-ratio
multiply-shift costs one #~"imul" plus one shift and beat the splitmix64
finalizer it replaces on both total steps and tail. The shift is not optional:
bit #~"i" of a product depends only on bits #~"0..i" of the operands, so a
multiply's low bits -- exactly the ones the mask reads -- are barely mixed,
while the high half carries every input bit.

#---: Methods

#~"get" / #~"getMut" / #~"set" / #~"has" / #~"keyAt" / #~"valueAt" /
#~"length" / #~"capacity" / #~"create", plus #~"iterate" on a set and
#~"iterateItems" on a map.
#_*"No" #~"remove" and #_*"no" #~"delete" -- if you need either, you want a
Map.

#~"getMut" is the one method a Map cannot offer. A Map's slot depends on a
hash #_*"of the key", so writing through a mutable view would leave the entry
in the wrong bucket; here the slot depends on the key alone, which a mutable
view of the #_*"value" does not touch. It earns its place on the value family,
whose #~"get" returns an #~"optionval" -- a copy -- so an update would
otherwise have to read the value out, change it and #~"set" it back. Both
forms declare it and share one emitted function, exactly as #~"ListRef.get" (a
view) and #~"ListVal.get" (a copy) already share one #~"getMut"; what differs
is the declared return.

The ordinary borrow rules still apply: two live views of one container
conflict, so a #~"getMut" result and a later lookup want separate scopes.

#~"keyAt" and #~"valueAt" read by #_*"position" rather than by key. Position
is a coordinate here in a way it is not in a Map: #~"entries" is dense and
insertion-ordered, so slot #~"i" is a plain array index and nothing can
perforate the sequence. They exist for the walk an iterator cannot serve --
one whose body has to reach the object that OWNS the container, which an
outstanding iterator borrow forbids for the whole loop. #~"keyAt" answers a
bare key, because the #~"idkey" bound makes one a scalar and #~"0" past the
end is the "no id" every id space already spells; #~"valueAt" answers exactly
what #~"get" answers, because a value is neither always a scalar nor always a
copy.


#--: string

#~"String" is a growable UTF-8 byte buffer. The struct lives on the stack; only
the data buffer is heap-allocated.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      uint64_t size;          /* byte count of current content */
      char* data;             /* heap-allocated data buffer */
      uint64_t capacity;      /* allocated buffer size */
  } z_string_t;
  """

Field order: #~"size" first, #~"data" second, #~"capacity" last. This matches
the field order of #~"List" (#~"length", #~"data", #~"capacity") for
consistency. The first two fields (#~"size", #~"data") match #~"StringView"
layout, enabling zero-cost casting. Putting #~"size" first also enables a
future small-string optimisation (SSO) where the high bit of #~"size" marks
inline storage and the remaining bytes of the struct store the string data
directly.

The struct (24 bytes) is on the stack. The #~"data" pointer references a
heap-allocated buffer. Strings are null-terminated (for C interop) but #~"size"
tracks the actual content length.

#---: Construction

#code language=c: """
  static z_string_t z_string_new(const char* s) {
      z_string_t str = {0};
      if (s) {
          str.size = strlen(s);
          str.capacity = str.size + 1;
          str.data = (char*)malloc(str.capacity);
          memcpy(str.data, s, str.capacity);  /* includes null terminator */
      }
      return str;
  }
  """

#---: Destructor

Frees the data buffer only:

#code language=c: """
  static void z_string_free(z_string_t* s) {
      if (s && s->data) free(s->data);
  }
  """

#---: Growth Formula

Unchanged: #~"new_cap = cap + (cap >> 1) + 16" (approximately 1.5x + 16).

#---: Runtime Functions

The string runtime functions and their signatures:

#table cols="4 6":
  Function                |Signature
  #~"z_string_new"        |Returns #~"z_string_t"
  #~"z_string_cat"        |Returns #~"z_string_t"; args are #~"z_string_t*"
  #~"z_string_from_i64"   |Returns #~"z_string_t"
  #~"z_string_from_f64"   |Returns #~"z_string_t"
  #~"z_string_free"       |Takes #~"z_string_t*"; frees #~"data" only (not struct)
  #~"z_string_eq"         |Takes two #~"z_string_t*" args
  #~"z_string_reserve"    |Takes #~"z_string_t*"
  #~"z_string_append"     |Takes #~"z_string_t*"
  #~"z_string_shrink"     |Takes #~"z_string_t*"
  #~"z_string_create"     |Returns #~"z_string_t"
  #~"z_string_print"      |Takes #~"z_string_t*"


#--: stringView

#~"StringView" is a #+ class: a read-only view into a #~"String", with
single-owner semantics and a #~".borrow" on the source string.

#---: Memory Layout

#code language=c: """
  typedef struct {
      uint64_t size;          /* byte count of the viewed region */
      const char* data;       /* pointer into string's data buffer */
  } z_stringview_t;
  """

Field order: #~"size" first, #~"data" second. This matches the first two
fields of #~"String" in order, name and type, enabling zero-cost casting from
#~"z_string_t*" to #~"z_stringview_t*".

#---: Ownership

Stringview is a class (reftype): single ownership, cannot be copied. It holds a
#~".borrow" on the source string (when created from a string variable).
Stringviews created from string literals point into static #~".rodata" and do
not lock anything (static data has infinite lifetime).

Stringview has no destructor (no heap allocations).

#---: Methods

#ul:
  #*: #~"length() out u64" -- byte count
  #*: #~"String() out String" -- materialise a new owned string from the view
  #*: #~"==(rhs: StringView) out bool" -- content comparison
  #*: #~"!=(rhs: StringView) out bool" -- negated comparison


#--: option / optionval -- Impact

#~"Option" is a union with subtypes #~"{ none: null, some: T }" (falsy arm
first). Under the new
model, the #~"Option" struct (#~"{ tag, void* }") moves to the stack but its
subtype data is still heap-allocated (standard union behaviour).

The nullable-pointer optimisation (#~"is_nullable_ptr") is preserved: for
pointer-typed payloads (box, protocol, etc.), #~"Option" is represented as a
bare pointer with #~"NULL" sentinel, avoiding the tag + void* overhead entirely.


#-: Born-Borrowed Analysis

#--: Removal from Valtypes

Born-borrowed is removed as a language concept. The non-owned field mechanism
is now available only to reftypes (classes). Valtypes (records, variants)
cannot hold locks.

#---: Rationale

The original motivation for born-borrowed was to allow stack-allocated records
to hold locked references to heap-allocated classes. Since records are
valtypes (freely copyable), the born-borrowed flag was needed to prevent copies
-- each copy would appear to hold a separate lock on the same target. This made
born-borrowed records neither truly values (they could not be copied) nor truly
references (they had no identity or destructor).

With classes now stack-allocated, the motivation disappears. A class with
non-owned fields has single-owner semantics that naturally prevent copies,
without any special born-borrowed mechanism. The class can be moved
(#~".take"), which born-borrowed records could not do.

#---: Migration

Types that were born-borrowed records become classes:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"StringView": becomes a class with #~".borrow" on the source string.]
  [#*: Iterators (eg. #~"BagIter"): become classes with #~".borrow" on the
  container.]
  [#*: Any record with non-owned fields: becomes a class.]

#---: Critique

Removing born-borrowed from valtypes is a simplification but has trade-offs:

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Pro: Cleaner type system. Valtypes are always values -- freely
  copyable, no identity, no destructor, no locks. There is no hybrid category.]
  [#*: #+ Pro: More flexible. Class-based views and iterators support
  #~".take" (moving to a new name), which born-borrowed records could not do.]
  [#*: #+ Pro: Consistent locking. Locks live only on types with identity
  (reftypes). This is conceptually cleaner -- a lock pins a specific instance,
  which requires identity.]
  [#*: #+ Con: Performance. Born-borrowed records were passed by value (struct
  copy) for #~".take" parameters, which is efficient for small structs (eg.
  stringView = 16 bytes). Classes are passed by pointer for #~".borrow"
  parameters, adding one level of indirection. However, the struct sizes are
  identical and the indirection cost is negligible.]
  [#*: #+ Con: Existing code. All existing born-borrowed records must be
  migrated to classes. This is a one-time migration cost. The compiler should
  emit clear errors guiding the migration.]
  [#*: #+ Con: Semantic shift. A #~"StringView" that was previously a value
  (cheaply passed around, no ownership tracking) becomes a reference (needs
  ownership tracking, borrow annotations). This adds some friction for common
  string-passing patterns. However, since born-borrowed stringviews were
  already restricted (could not be copied or stored), the practical difference
  is small.]
  [#*: #+ Issue: String literals. String literals currently produce
  stringviews pointing into #~".rodata". If stringView is a class, string
  literals produce an owned class instance. Since #~".rodata" has static
  lifetime, there is no safety concern, but the ownership tracking adds
  overhead to a very common pattern. Consider: should string literals remain a
  special case that produces a freely-borrowable stringView without locking?
  This would require the compiler to recognise that #~".rodata"-backed
  stringviews need no lock.]


#-: Pros, Cons, and Risks


#--: Pros

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Simpler mental model. "Everything is on the stack. Only box and data
  buffers are on the heap." Easy to teach and reason about.]
  [#*: #+ Fewer allocations. Most programs see fewer #~"malloc"/#~"free" calls,
  improving performance and reducing fragmentation.]
  [#*: #+ Unified field access. Local variables of all types use #~"." for
  field access. Only #~"this" in methods and boxed items use #~"->".]
  [#*: #+ Class non-owned fields. Enables natural class-based iterators and
  views. More flexible than the old born-borrowed record approach: a class
  instance supports #~".take" (moving to a new name).]
  [#*: #+ Clean type system. Valtypes are always values. Reftypes are always
  references with identity. No hybrid category.]
  [#*: #+ Predictable memory usage. Stack allocation makes memory usage more
  predictable and eliminates #~"malloc" failure risk for ordinary objects.]


#--: Cons

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Stack size. Large class structs consume stack space. For deeply
  recursive functions with large local classes, stack overflow is possible.
  Mitigation: #~"Box(T)" provides explicit opt-in to heap allocation.]
  [#*: #+ Struct copy cost. Moving a large class (via #~".take" or function
  return) copies the entire struct. NRVO mitigates returns, but #~".take"
  always copies. For large structs, #~"Box" should be preferred.]
  [#*: #+ Variant size. Inline tagged variants use the size of the largest
  subtype. For variants with subtypes of very different sizes, this wastes
  stack space. Rarely an issue in practice since variant subtypes must be
  valtypes (typically small).]
  [#*: #+ Recursive types. Self-referential types (linked list nodes, tree
  nodes) must use #~"Box(self)" for the recursive field. Error message should
  be clear.]


#--: Risks

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Protocol interaction. Protocol wrappers that own their data
  heap-allocate a copy of the implementation.]
  [#*: #+ C ABI for large structs. Some platforms pass large structs via hidden
  pointer at the ABI level. Handled transparently by the C compiler.]
  [#*: #+ String literal stringviews. Since stringView is a class, string
  literals produce owned class instances. The .rodata-backed
  stringviews are handled specially (no lock needed).]


#-: Future Directions

#ul:
  [#*: #+ Small-string optimisation (SSO). The #~"size" field is first in the
  string struct. The high bit can mark inline storage. When set, the remaining
  bytes of the struct (data pointer + capacity = 16 bytes) store the string
  data directly, avoiding a heap allocation for strings up to 15 bytes. The
  same #~"size-first" layout applies to #~"StringView", #~"List", and
  #~"ListView" for consistency.]
  [#*: #+ In-place list element creation. Construct elements directly in the
  data buffer without a stack intermediate.]
  [#*: #+ Box swap across types. Allow swapping #~"Box(T)" with #~"T" for the
  same underlying type.]
  [#*: #+ Automatic boxing. Compiler-inferred boxing when a class is passed to
  a function expecting #~"Box(T)".]
  [#*: #+ Move semantics. Elide struct copies when the compiler can prove the
  source is unused after the move.]
  [#*: #+ Arena allocation. Allow #~"Box" and #~"List" to allocate from a
  user-provided arena.]


#-: Verification Plan

After a change to the type representation, run:

#ol:
  [#*: #~"make check" -- formatting, linting, type checking.]
  [#*: #~"make test" -- the full test suite.]
  [#*: #~"make build" -- compile all examples to C and then to binaries.]
  [#*: Run compiled examples under ASan (#~"CFLAGS=-fsanitize=address make build")
  to detect memory errors, leaks, and undefined behaviour.]
  [#*: Manual inspection of generated C for a representative example
  to verify the emitted code matches the specification.]
